1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,671
Six forty in the morning, somewhere in

2
00:00:02,671 --> 00:00:03,936
the South Pacific.

3
00:00:03,936 --> 00:00:05,694
Your boat is upside down.

4
00:00:05,694 --> 00:00:07,732
And the only land you can see

5
00:00:07,732 --> 00:00:09,068
is a strip of sand.

6
00:00:09,348 --> 00:00:10,518
No phone signal.

7
00:00:10,518 --> 00:00:11,614
No fresh water.

8
00:00:11,614 --> 00:00:14,173
Nobody knows exactly where you are.

9
00:00:14,173 --> 00:00:16,951
Can you survive one hundred days here?

10
00:00:17,231 --> 00:00:18,962
Milo is about to find out.

11
00:00:18,962 --> 00:00:21,092
He just crawled out of the surf,

12
00:00:21,092 --> 00:00:23,489
still in his yellow raincoat, and he

13
00:00:23,489 --> 00:00:24,355
is not alone.

14
00:00:24,635 --> 00:00:26,906
Okay. Okay. The boat is gone, the

15
00:00:26,906 --> 00:00:28,834
radio is gone, and I think I

16
00:00:28,834 --> 00:00:30,554
swallowed half the ocean.

17
00:00:30,834 --> 00:00:32,624
That's Rosa, the skipper.

18
00:00:32,624 --> 00:00:34,056
She has one dry bag.

19
00:00:34,056 --> 00:00:36,705
Inside: a lighter, a plastic sheet, a

20
00:00:36,705 --> 00:00:39,210
small pot, and one bottle of water.

21
00:00:39,490 --> 00:00:41,997
Survival experts use a simple rule of

22
00:00:41,997 --> 00:00:44,639
three. You can last about three minutes

23
00:00:44,639 --> 00:00:45,452
without air,

24
00:00:45,732 --> 00:00:48,467
about three hours in harsh weather without

25
00:00:48,467 --> 00:00:51,332
shelter, three days without water, and three

26
00:00:51,332 --> 00:00:52,569
weeks without food.

27
00:00:52,849 --> 00:00:55,499
These are rough numbers, not a countdown.

28
00:00:55,499 --> 00:00:57,308
But they tell you the order.

29
00:00:57,308 --> 00:00:58,859
Shelter and water first.

30
00:00:58,859 --> 00:01:00,217
Food comes way later.

31
00:01:00,497 --> 00:01:02,878
So Milo does what every movie hero

32
00:01:02,878 --> 00:01:05,190
does first. He tries to drink the

33
00:01:05,190 --> 00:01:05,610
ocean.

34
00:01:05,890 --> 00:01:08,668
Milo, stop! Salt water makes you thirstier.

35
00:01:08,668 --> 00:01:09,443
Spit it out!

36
00:01:09,723 --> 00:01:12,175
She's right. According to the U.S.

37
00:01:12,175 --> 00:01:15,060
ocean agency NOAA, your kidneys can only

38
00:01:15,060 --> 00:01:17,512
make urine that is less salty than

39
00:01:17,512 --> 00:01:18,234
sea water.

40
00:01:18,514 --> 00:01:20,145
So to get rid of all that

41
00:01:20,145 --> 00:01:22,103
salt, your body has to pee out

42
00:01:22,103 --> 00:01:23,800
more water than you drank.

43
00:01:23,800 --> 00:01:25,693
You end up drier than before.

44
00:01:25,973 --> 00:01:27,516
Rule one on any island.

45
00:01:27,516 --> 00:01:28,993
Never drink sea water.

46
00:01:28,993 --> 00:01:30,872
Not a sip, not mixed in, not

47
00:01:30,872 --> 00:01:32,348
when you're desperate.

48
00:01:32,628 --> 00:01:34,539
Day two. The sun is the first

49
00:01:34,539 --> 00:01:36,252
thing trying to kill them.

50
00:01:36,252 --> 00:01:38,228
By noon the sand is hot enough

51
00:01:38,228 --> 00:01:39,414
to hurt bare feet.

52
00:01:39,694 --> 00:01:42,027
Sweating in direct sun can drain a

53
00:01:42,027 --> 00:01:44,291
lot of water from your body every

54
00:01:44,291 --> 00:01:46,349
hour. So shade is not comfort.

55
00:01:46,349 --> 00:01:48,407
Shade is water you don't lose.

56
00:01:48,686 --> 00:01:51,415
Milo and Rosa lean driftwood against a

57
00:01:51,415 --> 00:01:53,784
palm, lay palm fronds on top, and

58
00:01:53,784 --> 00:01:56,440
crawl under during the hottest hours.

59
00:01:56,720 --> 00:01:58,998
They move at dawn and dusk, when

60
00:01:58,998 --> 00:02:01,204
it's cool. In the middle of the

61
00:02:01,204 --> 00:02:02,272
day, they rest.

62
00:02:02,272 --> 00:02:03,624
Boring? Yes. Smart?

63
00:02:03,624 --> 00:02:04,265
Also yes.

64
00:02:04,545 --> 00:02:06,633
And they build it above the line

65
00:02:06,633 --> 00:02:08,525
of dried seaweed on the sand.

66
00:02:08,525 --> 00:02:10,678
That line shows how high the tide

67
00:02:10,678 --> 00:02:11,070
comes.

68
00:02:11,350 --> 00:02:13,361
Day three. The bottle is empty.

69
00:02:13,361 --> 00:02:15,503
Milo has a headache, his lips are

70
00:02:15,503 --> 00:02:17,385
cracked, and his pee is dark.

71
00:02:17,665 --> 00:02:21,205
Those are classic warning signs of dehydration.

72
00:02:21,205 --> 00:02:24,971
Headache, dizziness, dark urine, feeling confused.

73
00:02:24,971 --> 00:02:26,929
Water is now the only job.

74
00:02:27,209 --> 00:02:29,510
Then, at four in the afternoon, the

75
00:02:29,510 --> 00:02:30,496
sky turns grey.

76
00:02:30,776 --> 00:02:32,485
The sheet! Spread the sheet!

77
00:02:32,485 --> 00:02:33,584
Every drop counts!

78
00:02:33,864 --> 00:02:36,454
They stretch the plastic sheet between two

79
00:02:36,454 --> 00:02:38,428
sticks so it sags in the middle,

80
00:02:38,428 --> 00:02:40,340
and the rain runs into the pot.

81
00:02:40,620 --> 00:02:43,090
Fresh rain caught on a clean surface

82
00:02:43,090 --> 00:02:45,286
is one of the best water sources

83
00:02:45,286 --> 00:02:46,590
a castaway can get.

84
00:02:46,870 --> 00:02:49,424
Day four. They find green coconuts.

85
00:02:49,424 --> 00:02:51,979
The water inside a young coconut is

86
00:02:51,979 --> 00:02:54,022
safe to drink, and it helps.

87
00:02:54,022 --> 00:02:55,044
In moderation.

88
00:02:55,324 --> 00:02:57,160
But a stream they find in the

89
00:02:57,160 --> 00:02:58,934
jungle is a different story.

90
00:02:58,934 --> 00:03:01,277
Clear water can still carry germs you

91
00:03:01,277 --> 00:03:01,910
can't see.

92
00:03:02,190 --> 00:03:03,369
So they boil it.

93
00:03:03,369 --> 00:03:05,507
The CDC says bring water to a

94
00:03:05,507 --> 00:03:08,234
rolling boil for one minute, or three

95
00:03:08,234 --> 00:03:10,076
minutes at high altitude.

96
00:03:10,356 --> 00:03:12,431
That lighter in the dry bag just

97
00:03:12,431 --> 00:03:14,896
became the most valuable object on the

98
00:03:14,896 --> 00:03:15,349
island.

99
00:03:15,629 --> 00:03:17,047
Day five. A plane.

100
00:03:17,047 --> 00:03:19,172
Tiny, very high, very fast.

101
00:03:19,172 --> 00:03:21,771
Milo jumps and waves and screams.

102
00:03:21,771 --> 00:03:23,660
The plane does not care.

103
00:03:23,940 --> 00:03:25,779
From the air, one person on a

104
00:03:25,779 --> 00:03:27,237
beach looks like a dot.

105
00:03:27,237 --> 00:03:29,393
You have to make the island itself

106
00:03:29,393 --> 00:03:30,344
shout for help.

107
00:03:30,624 --> 00:03:34,244
Rescue groups like AdventureSmart teach this: three

108
00:03:34,244 --> 00:03:36,160
of anything means distress.

109
00:03:36,160 --> 00:03:39,425
Three fires, three whistle blasts, three piles

110
00:03:39,425 --> 00:03:40,064
of rocks.

111
00:03:40,344 --> 00:03:42,650
So they build three fire piles in

112
00:03:42,650 --> 00:03:44,537
a triangle, ready to light.

113
00:03:44,537 --> 00:03:47,333
Dry wood underneath, green leaves on top

114
00:03:47,333 --> 00:03:48,451
for thick smoke.

115
00:03:48,731 --> 00:03:51,220
Day six. Huge letters on the sand.

116
00:03:51,220 --> 00:03:53,196
S O S, made from dark rocks

117
00:03:53,196 --> 00:03:55,832
and seaweed so it stands out against

118
00:03:55,832 --> 00:03:57,003
the white beach.

119
00:03:57,283 --> 00:04:00,159
Big letters, sharp contrast, straight lines.

120
00:04:00,159 --> 00:04:03,100
Nature doesn't make straight lines, so pilots

121
00:04:03,100 --> 00:04:03,884
notice them.

122
00:04:04,164 --> 00:04:05,846
And Milo's secret weapon?

123
00:04:05,846 --> 00:04:08,200
His glasses case has a tiny mirror.

124
00:04:08,200 --> 00:04:10,286
A flash of sunlight can be seen

125
00:04:10,286 --> 00:04:12,775
much farther away than you can shout.

126
00:04:13,055 --> 00:04:15,589
Day nine. Rosa slips on the rocks.

127
00:04:15,589 --> 00:04:17,677
Sharp coral slices her shin.

128
00:04:17,957 --> 00:04:19,017
It's just a scratch.

129
00:04:19,017 --> 00:04:20,500
I'll rinse it in the sea and

130
00:04:20,500 --> 00:04:21,348
forget about it.

131
00:04:21,628 --> 00:04:23,770
On an island, small cuts are a

132
00:04:23,770 --> 00:04:26,055
big deal. Sea water and sand can

133
00:04:26,055 --> 00:04:28,982
carry bacteria, and there's no doctor for

134
00:04:28,982 --> 00:04:30,125
a hundred miles.

135
00:04:30,405 --> 00:04:32,593
So Milo cleans the cut with boiled

136
00:04:32,593 --> 00:04:35,039
water that has cooled down, and covers

137
00:04:35,039 --> 00:04:36,841
it with a clean strip of his

138
00:04:36,841 --> 00:04:37,871
raincoat lining.

139
00:04:38,151 --> 00:04:40,093
Then they watch it every day.

140
00:04:40,093 --> 00:04:42,705
Spreading redness, heat, pus or a fever

141
00:04:42,705 --> 00:04:45,184
are signs of infection that need real

142
00:04:45,184 --> 00:04:46,054
medical help.

143
00:04:46,334 --> 00:04:48,675
Day twelve. The cut is healing.

144
00:04:48,675 --> 00:04:50,110
Rosa owes Milo one.

145
00:04:50,110 --> 00:04:52,300
She does not say so out loud.

146
00:04:52,580 --> 00:04:55,018
Day fourteen. Their stomachs are loud.

147
00:04:55,018 --> 00:04:57,070
And the jungle is full of bright

148
00:04:57,070 --> 00:04:57,584
berries.

149
00:04:57,864 --> 00:05:00,512
This is where many survival stories go

150
00:05:00,512 --> 00:05:02,882
wrong. Never eat a plant, berry or

151
00:05:02,882 --> 00:05:05,670
mushroom you can't identify for certain.

152
00:05:05,950 --> 00:05:09,071
Some poisonous plants look exactly like safe

153
00:05:09,071 --> 00:05:11,695
ones. And remember the rule of three.

154
00:05:11,695 --> 00:05:13,823
You can go weeks without food.

155
00:05:13,823 --> 00:05:16,022
Poison can end things in hours.

156
00:05:16,302 --> 00:05:18,049
So they stick to the sea.

157
00:05:18,049 --> 00:05:19,167
Rosa knows fish.

158
00:05:19,167 --> 00:05:21,472
They wade in shallow water at low

159
00:05:21,472 --> 00:05:23,498
tide and trap small fish in a

160
00:05:23,498 --> 00:05:24,476
ring of rocks.

161
00:05:24,757 --> 00:05:26,037
Crabs at night, too.

162
00:05:26,037 --> 00:05:28,726
Everything gets cooked all the way through

163
00:05:28,726 --> 00:05:29,495
on the fire.

164
00:05:29,495 --> 00:05:32,056
Raw seafood can make you seriously sick.

165
00:05:32,336 --> 00:05:34,025
This crab is looking at me.

166
00:05:34,025 --> 00:05:34,714
I feel bad.

167
00:05:34,714 --> 00:05:35,965
I'm still eating it.

168
00:05:36,245 --> 00:05:37,389
Day twenty one.

169
00:05:37,389 --> 00:05:38,685
The wind changes.

170
00:05:38,685 --> 00:05:41,276
Clouds pile up on the horizon like

171
00:05:41,276 --> 00:05:42,191
a dark wall.

172
00:05:42,471 --> 00:05:44,658
Grab the pot, the sheet, the lighter.

173
00:05:44,658 --> 00:05:47,259
Everything that matters goes to high ground,

174
00:05:47,259 --> 00:05:47,850
right now!

175
00:05:48,130 --> 00:05:50,768
In a tropical storm, the danger is

176
00:05:50,768 --> 00:05:51,855
not just wind.

177
00:05:51,855 --> 00:05:54,726
It's water. Waves and storm surge can

178
00:05:54,726 --> 00:05:56,433
wash over a low beach.

179
00:05:56,713 --> 00:05:58,566
They wait it out behind a big

180
00:05:58,566 --> 00:06:00,610
rock on the highest point of the

181
00:06:00,610 --> 00:06:02,974
island, away from tall trees that can

182
00:06:02,974 --> 00:06:04,699
drop branches and coconuts.

183
00:06:04,979 --> 00:06:06,994
Morning. The shelter is gone.

184
00:06:06,994 --> 00:06:08,592
The SOS is half buried.

185
00:06:08,592 --> 00:06:11,024
But the lighter is dry, and they're

186
00:06:11,024 --> 00:06:11,788
both alive.

187
00:06:12,068 --> 00:06:14,751
Day thirty. Here's a part nobody shows

188
00:06:14,751 --> 00:06:17,221
in movies. The hardest enemy now is

189
00:06:17,221 --> 00:06:18,280
not the island.

190
00:06:18,280 --> 00:06:20,398
It's the boredom and the fear.

191
00:06:20,678 --> 00:06:23,720
Survival trainers say a routine helps.

192
00:06:23,720 --> 00:06:24,841
Water at dawn.

193
00:06:24,841 --> 00:06:26,443
Fishing at low tide.

194
00:06:26,443 --> 00:06:27,724
Fix the signals.

195
00:06:27,724 --> 00:06:28,765
Rest at noon.

196
00:06:28,765 --> 00:06:29,806
Fire at dusk.

197
00:06:30,086 --> 00:06:32,216
Milo scratches a line on a palm

198
00:06:32,216 --> 00:06:33,591
trunk every morning.

199
00:06:33,591 --> 00:06:34,690
It's a calendar.

200
00:06:34,690 --> 00:06:37,027
It's also proof that he's still in

201
00:06:37,027 --> 00:06:37,508
charge.

202
00:06:37,788 --> 00:06:39,657
Rosa, rate my sand castle.

203
00:06:39,657 --> 00:06:41,956
Be honest. Actually no, be nice.

204
00:06:42,236 --> 00:06:43,157
Six out of ten.

205
00:06:43,157 --> 00:06:44,446
The tower is leaning.

206
00:06:44,726 --> 00:06:45,823
Day forty seven.

207
00:06:45,823 --> 00:06:48,018
While Milo is wading for fish, a

208
00:06:48,018 --> 00:06:50,213
grey fin cuts through the water.

209
00:06:50,493 --> 00:06:51,689
Calm. Don't splash.

210
00:06:51,689 --> 00:06:53,767
Back out slowly, face it, and get

211
00:06:53,767 --> 00:06:54,837
out of the water.

212
00:06:55,117 --> 00:06:56,951
Shark bites are very rare.

213
00:06:56,951 --> 00:07:00,338
But wildlife experts advise avoiding murky water

214
00:07:00,338 --> 00:07:02,807
and swimming at dawn and dusk, when

215
00:07:02,807 --> 00:07:04,006
many sharks feed.

216
00:07:04,286 --> 00:07:06,566
From that day on, Milo fishes only

217
00:07:06,566 --> 00:07:09,181
in the shallows, in daylight, and never

218
00:07:09,181 --> 00:07:09,584
alone.

219
00:07:09,864 --> 00:07:10,935
Day sixty one.

220
00:07:10,935 --> 00:07:12,390
A brutal heat wave.

221
00:07:12,390 --> 00:07:13,844
No rain for a week.

222
00:07:13,844 --> 00:07:16,064
Now Rosa is the one who looks

223
00:07:16,064 --> 00:07:16,524
wrong.

224
00:07:16,804 --> 00:07:18,308
She stopped sweating.

225
00:07:18,308 --> 00:07:20,028
Her skin is hot and dry.

226
00:07:20,028 --> 00:07:21,676
She's talking nonsense.

227
00:07:21,676 --> 00:07:24,112
Those can be signs of heat stroke,

228
00:07:24,112 --> 00:07:25,330
a real emergency.

229
00:07:25,610 --> 00:07:27,984
The CDC's advice is to cool the

230
00:07:27,984 --> 00:07:30,819
person fast. Milo drags her into deep

231
00:07:30,819 --> 00:07:33,729
shade, soaks her clothes, and fans her

232
00:07:33,729 --> 00:07:35,108
with a palm frond.

233
00:07:35,388 --> 00:07:38,246
By night, she's sitting up, sipping coconut

234
00:07:38,246 --> 00:07:39,442
water. She's okay.

235
00:07:39,442 --> 00:07:41,701
They ration every drop after that.

236
00:07:41,981 --> 00:07:43,144
Day eighty eight.

237
00:07:43,144 --> 00:07:44,992
A ship. A real ship, on the

238
00:07:44,992 --> 00:07:45,539
horizon.

239
00:07:45,819 --> 00:07:47,294
The fires! Light the fires!

240
00:07:47,294 --> 00:07:48,440
Where is the lighter?

241
00:07:48,720 --> 00:07:50,536
But the wood is damp from the

242
00:07:50,536 --> 00:07:52,729
night. By the time the smoke rises,

243
00:07:52,729 --> 00:07:53,793
the ship is gone.

244
00:07:54,073 --> 00:07:55,890
Lesson learned the hard way.

245
00:07:55,890 --> 00:07:58,422
Signal fires only work if they're ready

246
00:07:58,422 --> 00:08:00,953
in seconds. Keep dry tinder covered and

247
00:08:00,953 --> 00:08:02,705
the lighter in your pocket.

248
00:08:02,985 --> 00:08:05,174
From now on, Milo sleeps with the

249
00:08:05,174 --> 00:08:07,827
lighter. Rosa covers the tinder with the

250
00:08:07,827 --> 00:08:10,016
plastic sheet every single night.

251
00:08:10,296 --> 00:08:11,436
Day ninety nine.

252
00:08:11,436 --> 00:08:13,929
A low humming sound from the north.

253
00:08:13,929 --> 00:08:15,496
Milo knows that sound.

254
00:08:15,776 --> 00:08:18,311
Rosa lights all three fires in under

255
00:08:18,311 --> 00:08:20,422
a minute. Green leaves on top.

256
00:08:20,422 --> 00:08:22,464
Three thick columns of smoke.

257
00:08:22,744 --> 00:08:24,245
Milo aims the mirror.

258
00:08:24,245 --> 00:08:25,674
Flash. Flash. Flash.

259
00:08:25,674 --> 00:08:27,890
Three flashes, again and again.

260
00:08:28,170 --> 00:08:29,605
The helicopter turns.

261
00:08:29,605 --> 00:08:30,698
It circles once.

262
00:08:30,698 --> 00:08:31,860
It's coming down.

263
00:08:32,140 --> 00:08:34,649
Coast guard. We saw your smoke and

264
00:08:34,649 --> 00:08:35,387
the S O S.

265
00:08:35,387 --> 00:08:36,494
Is anyone hurt?

266
00:08:36,774 --> 00:08:37,879
Day one hundred.

267
00:08:37,879 --> 00:08:40,229
Sunburned, skinny, and very alive.

268
00:08:40,509 --> 00:08:43,152
So, could you survive one hundred days

269
00:08:43,152 --> 00:08:44,473
on a desert island?

270
00:08:44,473 --> 00:08:47,046
Here's what kept Milo and Rosa alive.

271
00:08:47,326 --> 00:08:49,158
One. Never drink sea water.

272
00:08:49,158 --> 00:08:52,143
Two. Shade before anything, because sweat is

273
00:08:52,143 --> 00:08:53,161
water you lose.

274
00:08:53,441 --> 00:08:56,070
Three. Catch rain, and boil other water

275
00:08:56,070 --> 00:08:57,283
for a full minute.

276
00:08:57,283 --> 00:08:59,912
Four. Three of anything means help, and

277
00:08:59,912 --> 00:09:02,136
signals must be ready in seconds.

278
00:09:02,416 --> 00:09:05,549
Five. Treat small cuts like big problems.

279
00:09:05,549 --> 00:09:08,759
And never eat anything you can't identify.

280
00:09:08,759 --> 00:09:09,905
Hunger is slow.

281
00:09:09,905 --> 00:09:11,051
Poison is fast.

